  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved skills and decent employment for youth and vulnerable groups</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The strategy focuses on a multifaceted approach towards a job-rich economic recovery and development. It includes job creation, regulatory frameworks, demographic challenges, youth unemployment, gender disparity, and the need for skill development to tap into the demographic dividend. 
Firstly, the creation of productive, remunerative, and decent employment must be prioritized. This requires strengthening a regulatory framework that provides flexibility for adjusting to changing labour market dynamics, ensuring just transitions for all. A well-functioning, equitable, and rights-based labour market is crucial for creating an attractive investment climate for new job opportunities. Additionally, efforts should be made to address the estimated 4.6 million jobs lost due to recent floods through targeted job creation initiatives.
The rapid population growth in Pakistan, currently at 2.55%, has created a significant gap between the supply and demand of workers. To manage this, the strategy should include measures to absorb the growing labour force, aiming for a 6 to 7% GDP growth. This can be achieved by designing policies to promote self-employment schemes, support the private sector, and address the skills mismatch that prevents harnessing the demographic dividend effectively.
Youth unemployment and the significant rate of young people not in education, employment, or training (NEET) are critical issues that need urgent attention. Policies should be designed to promote self-employed schemes and private sector engagement to enhance job opportunities for the youth. Investing in human capital is crucial for enhancing productivity and tapping into the demographic dividends. This includes revamping education systems to align with market demands, offering vocational training programs,and emphasizing continuous learning to adapt to evolving industries. Moreover, massive investments are required in skilling, upskilling, and reskilling to prepare the workforce for the future.</narrative>
  </description>
